# Plan vs build models

Same split as sabaki.dance. The September 2026 plan commit that shipped Correct / Format / Rephrase without a separate approval is why this is a rule, not a suggestion.

| Job | Model | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Propose (usage / model-audit / growth / architect) | Claude Opus 5 | Judgement. Cursor proposals have been bad. |
| Plan (implementer plan step, interactive plans) | Claude Opus 5 (`claude-opus-5`) | Decides HOW. Writes one plan file, no code. |
| Build (implementer, "implement this") | Cursor Grok 4.6 (`cursor-grok-4.6-high`) | Types the code from a plan. |
| Judge (implementer review) | Claude Opus 5 | Builder never judges its own diff. |

**In an interactive Cursor session (this is Grok):**

- Asked to **propose, plan, audit, or design** a change: do not write the plan yourself. Launch a Task subagent with `model` `claude-opus-5-thinking-high`. You may implement only after that plan exists and the user accepts it.
- Asked to **implement** something already decided (including "take this out", "do what we just agreed"): implement it here. Do not spawn Opus to type the code.

Scheduled jobs pin the same IDs. Do not switch a review job to Cursor or Sonnet, and do not switch the implementer builder to `auto`.
